from chemistry_helpers import TOPICS
from chemistry_qa_writer import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'How many particles are in one mole of a substance?','6.02 × 10²³.','4.3.2.1'),
q(1,'Convert 40.0 cm³ to dm³.','0.0400 dm³.','4.3.4'),
q(1,'What is a limiting reactant?','The reactant completely used up, which limits the amount of product.','4.3.2.4'),
q(1,'State the volume of one mole of gas at room temperature and pressure.','24 dm³.','4.3.5'),
q(2,'Calculate the relative formula mass of Ca(OH)₂. Aᵣ: Ca = 40, O = 16, H = 1.','- Mᵣ = 40 + 2 × (16 + 1).\n- **Mᵣ = 74**, with no unit.','4.3.1.2'),
q(2,'Explain why magnesium gains mass when it burns in air.','- Magnesium **reacts with oxygen from the air**.\n- The magnesium oxide includes the mass of this oxygen as well as the original magnesium.','4.3.1.3'),
q(2,'Explain why an open flask containing acid and carbonate loses mass during the reaction.','- **Carbon dioxide gas forms and escapes**.\n- Its mass is no longer measured with the flask, although total mass including the gas is conserved.','4.3.1.3'),
q(2,'Calculate the amount of substance in 5.6 g iron. Aᵣ: Fe = 56.','- n = mass ÷ molar mass = 5.6 ÷ 56.\n- **0.10 mol**.','4.3.2.1'),
q(2,'Calculate the mass of 0.15 mol sodium hydroxide, NaOH. Aᵣ: Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1.','- Mᵣ = 40; mass = 0.15 × 40.\n- **6.0 g**.','4.3.2.1'),
q(2,'A reaction has theoretical yield 12.0 g and actual yield 9.0 g. Calculate the percentage yield.','- Yield = (9.0 ÷ 12.0) × 100.\n- **75%**.','4.3.3.1'),
q(2,'Give two reasons a reaction may produce less than its theoretical yield.','- Some product is **lost during transfer or separation**.\n- A **reversible reaction may not go to completion**.','4.3.3.1'),
q(2,'A solution contains 3.0 g solute in 150 cm³. Calculate its concentration in g/dm³.','- Volume = 0.150 dm³.\n- c = 3.0 ÷ 0.150 = **20 g/dm³**.','4.3.2.5'),
q(2,'The mass of solute is doubled and the final solution volume is halved. How does concentration change? Explain.','- Concentration becomes **four times greater**.\n- c = mass/volume, so the factor is 2 ÷ 0.5 = 4.','4.3.2.5; 4.3.4','2023 Q05.2'),
q(3,'Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in Mg(NO₃)₂. Aᵣ: Mg = 24, N = 14, O = 16.','- Mᵣ = 24 + 2 × (14 + 3 × 16) = **148**.\n- Oxygen contributes 6 × 16 = **96**.\n- Percentage = 96/148 × 100 = **64.9%** to 3 significant figures.','4.3.1.2'),
q(3,'Calculate the number of molecules in 8.8 g carbon dioxide. Mᵣ: CO₂ = 44.','- Moles = 8.8 ÷ 44 = **0.20 mol**.\n- Molecules = 0.20 × 6.02 × 10²³.\n- **1.204 × 10²³ molecules**.','4.3.2.1','2022 Q03.5'),
q(3,'Repeated temperature rises are 8.2, 8.4 and 8.6 °C. Calculate the mean and estimate uncertainty as half the range. Explain whether repeating removes an incorrectly calibrated thermometer error.','- Mean = **8.4 °C**.\n- Half-range = (8.6 − 8.2)/2 = **0.2 °C**; report 8.4 ± 0.2 °C.\n- Repeating does **not remove systematic calibration error**.','4.3.1.4','2023 Q02.5–02.6'),
q(3,'Calculate the mass of sodium hydroxide in 200 cm³ of 0.250 mol/dm³ solution. Mᵣ: NaOH = 40.','- Volume = **0.200 dm³**.\n- n = cV = 0.250 × 0.200 = **0.0500 mol**.\n- Mass = 0.0500 × 40 = **2.00 g**.','4.3.4'),
q(3,'Calculate the volume of 7.0 g nitrogen gas at RTP. Mᵣ: N₂ = 28.','- n = 7.0/28 = **0.25 mol**.\n- V = n × 24.\n- **6.0 dm³**, or 6,000 cm³.','4.3.5','2019 Q07.7'),
q(3,'Calcium carbonate decomposes: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Calculate the atom economy for producing CaO. Mᵣ: CaCO₃ = 100, CaO = 56, CO₂ = 44.','- Desired-product formula mass = **56**.\n- Total reactant formula mass = **100**.\n- Atom economy = 56/100 × 100 = **56%**.','4.3.3.2','2023 Q09.6'),
q(4,'Find the mass of aluminium obtained from 20.4 g Al₂O₃. Equation: 2Al₂O₃ → 4Al + 3O₂. Aᵣ: Al = 27, O = 16. Assume complete conversion.','- Mᵣ Al₂O₃ = 2 × 27 + 3 × 16 = **102**.\n- Moles Al₂O₃ = 20.4/102 = **0.200 mol**.\n- Ratio 2 : 4 gives **0.400 mol Al**.\n- Mass Al = 0.400 × 27 = **10.8 g**.','4.3.2.2','2019 Q07.5; 2022 Q07.4'),
q(4,'3.2 g methane reacts with 12.8 g oxygen to produce 8.8 g carbon dioxide and 7.2 g water. Deduce the balanced equation. Mᵣ: CH₄ = 16, O₂ = 32, CO₂ = 44, H₂O = 18.','- Moles: CH₄ 0.20; O₂ 0.40; CO₂ 0.20; H₂O 0.40.\n- Divide by 0.20 to get **1 : 2 : 1 : 2**.\n- **CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O**.\n- Atom totals and masses balance: 16.0 g reactants gives 16.0 g products.','4.3.2.3','2019 Q08.4'),
q(4,'Nitrogen and hydrogen react: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. Mix 0.20 mol N₂ with 0.30 mol H₂. Identify the limiting reactant and calculate the amount of ammonia and the amount of nitrogen left.','- 0.30 mol H₂ needs 0.30/3 = **0.10 mol N₂**, less than the 0.20 mol available.\n- **Hydrogen is limiting**.\n- Ammonia formed = 0.30 × 2/3 = **0.20 mol**.\n- Nitrogen left = 0.20 − 0.10 = **0.10 mol**.','4.3.2.4','2022 Q07.6'),
q(4,'At the same temperature and pressure, 80 cm³ hydrogen is mixed with 50 cm³ oxygen and ignited. Equation: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Calculate the oxygen volume left after reaction and explain why the equation does not give the volume of liquid water.','- 80 cm³ H₂ needs **40 cm³ O₂**.\n- Hydrogen is limiting, so oxygen left = 50 − 40 = **10 cm³**.\n- Gas volume ratios equal mole ratios at the **same temperature and pressure**.\n- Liquid water is not a gas, so the gas-volume rule cannot give its volume.','4.3.5','2022 Q07.6'),
q(4,'A student heats 20.0 g CaCO₃ and collects 8.4 g CaO. Calculate the percentage yield. Equation: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Mᵣ: CaCO₃ = 100, CaO = 56.','- CaCO₃ moles = 20.0/100 = **0.200 mol**.\n- CaO theoretical moles = 0.200; theoretical mass = 0.200 × 56 = **11.2 g**.\n- Yield = 8.4/11.2 × 100.\n- **75%**.','4.3.3.1'),
q(4,'For X₂O₃ + 3H₂ → 2X + 3H₂O, the atom economy for X is 75.0%. Calculate Aᵣ of X. Aᵣ: H = 1, O = 16.','- Let Aᵣ of X = x. Desired mass from equation = **2x**; reactant total = **2x + 54**.\n- 2x/(2x + 54) = 0.750.\n- 2x = 1.5x + 40.5, so 0.5x = 40.5.\n- **x = 81.0**.','4.3.3.2','2023 Q09.6'),
q(5,'25.0 cm³ of 0.0800 mol/dm³ Na₂CO₃ reacts exactly with 20.0 cm³ HCl. Calculate the HCl concentration in mol/dm³ and g/dm³. Equation: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + CO₂ + H₂O. Mᵣ: HCl = 36.5.','- Na₂CO₃ volume = **0.0250 dm³**.\n- Moles Na₂CO₃ = 0.0800 × 0.0250 = **0.00200 mol**.\n- Equation ratio gives **0.00400 mol HCl**.\n- HCl concentration = 0.00400/0.0200 = **0.200 mol/dm³**.\n- Mass concentration = 0.200 × 36.5 = **7.30 g/dm³**.','4.3.4','2019 Q09.5; 2022 Q08.3; 2023 Q05.5'),
q(6,'Titanium dioxide reacts: TiO₂ + 2Cl₂ + 2C → TiCl₄ + 2CO. Calculate the volume of chlorine at RTP needed for 40.0 kg TiO₂. Aᵣ: Ti = 48, O = 16. One mole of gas occupies 24 dm³.','- Convert 40.0 kg to **40,000 g**.\n- Mᵣ TiO₂ = 48 + 2 × 16 = **80**.\n- Moles TiO₂ = 40,000/80 = **500 mol**.\n- Ratio TiO₂ : Cl₂ = 1 : 2.\n- Chlorine amount = **1,000 mol**.\n- Volume = 1,000 × 24 = **24,000 dm³**.','4.3.5','2023 Q10.2'),
q(6,'Route A has atom economy 90%, yield 60% and takes 3 hours. Route B has atom economy 70%, yield 95% and takes 20 minutes; its by-product can be sold. Evaluate which route a factory should choose.','- A directs a larger proportion of starting atoms into the desired product, so its **theoretical waste is lower**.\n- Its lower yield means less expected product is actually collected; recoverable reactants and losses matter.\n- B has a **higher yield and faster production**, potentially improving throughput and cost.\n- Selling B’s by-product reduces disposal and may provide income, despite its lower atom economy for the main product.\n- Compare feedstock, energy, separation and disposal costs and whether a market exists for the by-product.\n- B is a reasonable provisional choice from these data, but a final choice needs those costs and environmental impacts; atom economy alone is insufficient.','4.3.3.1–4.3.3.2')]
write_bank(TOPICS[2],'Quantitative chemistry',items,'- Reacting masses and mole ratios: 2019 Q07.5 and Q08.4; 2022 Q07.4.\n- Titration calculations: 2019 Q09.5; 2022 Q08.3; 2023 Q05.5.\n- Gas amounts and ratios: 2019 Q07.7; 2022 Q07.6; 2023 Q10.2.\n- Additional sampled themes: uncertainty (2023 Q02.5), particle counts (2022 Q03.5), atom economy algebra (2023 Q09.6).\n\nThe first three themes occur across the sample; the others are examples, not frequency claims. Remaining practice covers current specification requirements, including yield. Values, prompts and mark allocations are original.')
